.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SYSFS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysfs
.Nd get information about the file system types
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ft int
.Fn sysfs "int option" "const char *fsname"
.Ft int
.Fn sysfs "int option" "unsigned int fs_index" "char *buf"
.Ft int
.Fn sysfs "int option"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn sysfs
numbers the file system types the kernel knows from 0, in the order
.Pa /proc/filesystems
lists them, and answers by
.Fa option :
.Bl -tag -width 1n
.It 1
The index of the type named by the string
.Fa fsname .
.It 2
The name of the type whose index is
.Fa fs_index ,
copied with its terminating NUL into
.Fa buf ,
which must be large enough.
.It 3
The number of types.
.El
.Pp
The call has nothing to do with the
.Pa /sys
file system that
.Xr sysfs 5
describes.
.Ss System call numbers
The call is 135 on i386 and 139 on x86-64.
The x86-64 kernel has it for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80
too, as
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Option 1 returns the index, option 2 zero and option 3 the number of
types.
On error the call returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/oldfsprobe.c
reads
.Pa /proc/filesystems ,
checks that option 3 gives the number of its lines and that options 1
and 2 give each line's name and index both ways, and checks each error.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa option
is not 1, 2 or 3;
.Fa fsname
names no type; or
.Fa fs_index
is not below the number of types.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
.Fa fsname
is the empty string.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
.Fa fsname
has no NUL in its first 4096 bytes.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa fsname
or
.Fa buf
is outside the caller's memory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr ustat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The call is Linux's, which builds it unless
.Dv CONFIG_SYSFS_SYSCALL
is off, and its rules are those of Linux 6.12.
No C library has a wrapper; a program calls it through
.Xr syscall 2 .
